#+BRANCH: feat-check-test-suite-membership
#+DATE: 2026-09-27

* Observed

From the write-up of =sysvar-table-ignores-runtime-dialect-change= (closed),
which found it while adding tests and named it the worst of the three bugs it
hit:

#+begin_quote
*Un fichier de tests sans =(in-suite …)= compile, se charge, et ne tourne pas.*
Mes quatre tests ont d'abord été déclarés hors suite : la suite est passée VERTE
en ne les exécutant pas, et je l'ai d'abord lue comme une réussite. C'est le pire
des trois, parce qu'il se présente comme un succès. La convention du dépôt —
chaque fichier de tests redéclare =(in-suite …)= — n'est donc pas décorative, et
le harnais gagnerait à refuser un fichier de tests qui n'en a pas.
#+end_quote

That last clause is this ticket. Filed as its own issue rather than left as a
sentence in a closed one, per AGENTS.md.

* Why it matters more than it looks

Every other failure in this repository announces itself. This one *reports
success*: the file compiles, the tests are defined, nothing warns, and the suite
is green because it ran fewer tests than the author believes. It is the same
family as the release check that passed a 42 MB archive no launcher could open,
and as the nightly sweep that printed "nothing to sweep" for weeks — and this
repository keeps meeting that family.

It is also easy to reintroduce: a new test file is usually copied from a
neighbour, and the one line that does not look load-bearing is the one that is.

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.132

=scripts/check-test-suite-membership.py=, =make check-test-suite-membership=,
wired into the CI documentation lane (seconds, in a lane that always runs).
Three rules, each because its absence is silent:

1. A file with =(test NAME ...)= forms must carry =(in-suite ...)=. Without it
   the tests attach to whatever suite happened to be current at load time — in
   practice nothing that gets run.
2. The suite it names must be declared by a =def-suite= in the same =tests/=
   directory. A typo names a suite that does not exist; a name that happens to
   match ANOTHER subproject's suite would not even signal.
3. A =tests/= directory must not declare more than one ROOT suite (a =def-suite=
   with no =:in=). A second root is not reachable from the one the runner runs,
   so its tests load and never execute — rule 1's silence, one level up.

State of the tree when the check landed: *24 test directories, 106 files defining
tests, all clean.* So it is a guard against the next occurrence, not a cleanup —
which is the right moment to add one.

** The check is tested, and each rule watched failing

=scripts/tests/test-check-test-suite-membership.py=, 8 tests. The script takes
=--root= for exactly this: every rule is exercised against a fixture that
violates it AND one that does not, in pairs — a file without =(in-suite ...)=
fails and the same file with it passes; two root suites fail and a =:in= child
passes. A check nobody has watched fail proves nothing, and this one guards a
defect that already passed for green once.

** What is NOT checked, and why it is stated rather than left as a gap

Whether the runner actually runs the root suite, and whether a child suite is
reachable through =:in=. Those need the live FiveAM registry rather than the
source text. The cheap static half runs in every pipeline; the dynamic
complement — walk every defined test and assert it is reachable from the suite
the runner runs — would catch the remaining shape and is worth doing if this
ever bites again.

=autolisp-test/= is excluded: its corpus harness has its own =deftest= macros and
its own runner, so FiveAM's suite rules do not apply there. The exclusion is
tested too, so it cannot quietly widen.

* Note

Found while triaging =autolisp-globals-writability-triage= (whose dependency is
the ticket quoted above). The triage itself needed no work — it already contains
its answer — but its dependency's post-mortem contained an unimplemented
recommendation, which is the kind of thing that otherwise stays buried in a
closed file.
